        "detail": "AC2 family \"fragment-control-sequences\" is generated only inside another family's target; no dedicated target, corpus, or campaign bound of its own: Fragment and control sequences ARE generated -- draw_frame in adversarial_fuzz.rs draws continuation/text/binary/close/ping/pong and reserved opcodes, clears FIN one time in five, sets RSV bits, and draw_stream concatenates one to ten such frames with optional garbage tails and truncations. But they are generated only INSIDE the frame-decode target: this family has no target of its own, no campaign bound of its own, and no separately captured artifact. Recorded as shared coverage, not as a dedicated target, so the record cannot be read as more than it is."
      },
      {
        "code": "FUZZ_TARGET_ABSENT",
        "disposition": "BLOCK",
        "target": "handshake-client",
        "detail": "AC2 family \"handshake-client\" has no generative fuzz target: rust/ws-core/fuzz-seeds/us010 holds 11 committed handshake-client seeds and rust/ws-core/tests/handshake_seeds.rs replays them at FIXED expectations. That is a regression corpus, not a fuzz target: no byte of handshake input is ever GENERATED. rust/ws-core/tests/adversarial_fuzz.rs contains zero handshake drives (its only occurrence of the word is the MaxHandshakeBytes limit field). rust/ws-core/tests/handshake_client.rs is fixed-example unit testing. No generator, no campaign, no artifact capture, no replay of a generated case exists for ClientHandshake."
      },
      {
        "code": "FUZZ_TARGET_ABSENT",
        "disposition": "BLOCK",
        "target": "handshake-server",
        "detail": "AC2 family \"handshake-server\" has no generative fuzz target: Same shape as handshake-client one parser over: rust/ws-core/fuzz-seeds/us011 holds 17 committed server seeds replayed at fixed expectations by rust/ws-core/tests/handshake_seeds.rs, and rust/ws-core/tests/handshake_server.rs is fixed-example unit testing. ServerHandshake never receives a generated request. Note that family_seed_corpus_anchors in adversarial_fuzz.rs DOES read the us010/us011 hex files, but it feeds them to ConnectionCore as post-handshake FRAME bytes; that exercises the frame decoder over handshake-shaped bytes and gives the handshake parsers no coverage at all."
      },
      {
        "code": "FUZZ_TARGET_ABSENT",
        "disposition": "BLOCK",
        "target": "owner-driver-command-byte-schedules",
        "detail": "AC2 family \"owner-driver-command-byte-schedules\" has no generative fuzz target: Nothing generates command/byte schedules at the ws-driver seam. rust/ws-driver/tests/schedule_exploration.rs EXHAUSTIVELY ENUMERATES every interleaving of five fixed actor programs within a context-switch bound -- systematic, deterministic, and valuable, but not generative fuzzing: it draws nothing and has no random or evolving input. The retained rust/ws-driver/fuzz-seeds/us017 corpus (16 files) is consumed as byte-for-byte EXPECTED OUTPUT of the minimizer, not as fuzz seeds fed to a generator. family_command_interleave in adversarial_fuzz.rs does interleave generated commands with bytes, but at the ws-core seam, against ConnectionCore, with no ConnectionDriver, no owner poll loop, and no transport write path -- a different seam from the one AC2 names."
